Norgolith content schemas system allows you to define and enforce structure and validation rules for your Norg documents’ metadata. This ensures consistency across your content and prevents errors during the build process.

** Why Use Content Schemas?
   Content schemas are particularly useful for:
   - *Enforcing consistency*: Ensure all posts have required fields like `title`, `author`, or `publish_date`.
   - *Preventing errors*: Catch missing or invalid metadata before building your site.
   - *Conditional logic*: Require specific fields based on other metadata (e.g., require `hero_image` if `featured = true`).
   - *Team collaboration*: Provide clear guidelines for content contributors.

   +html.class text-base-alt mt-12 lg:mt-14
   ___

** Schema Basics
   Content schemas are defined in your `norgolith.toml` file under the `[content_schema]` section. A schema consists of:
   - *Required fields*: Fields that must be present in the metadata.
   - *Field definitions*: Rules for validating individual fields.
   - *Validation rules*: Conditional logic for enforcing additional constraints.

*** Example: Basic Schema
    @code toml
    [content_schema]
    required = ["title", "author"]

    [content_schema.fields.title]
    type = "string"
    max_length = 120

    [content_schema.fields.author]
    type = "string"
    @end

** Field Types
   Norgolith supports several field types, each with its own validation rules:

*** String
    - Validates text fields.

**** Options
     - `min_length`: Minimum allowed characters.
     - `max_length`: Maximum allowed characters.
     - `pattern`: Regex pattern for validation.

**** Example
     @code toml
     [content_schema.fields.title]
     type = "string"
     max_length = 100
     pattern = "^[A-Z].*"  # Must start with uppercase letter
     @end

*** Integer
    - Validates whole numbers.

**** Options
     - `min`: Minimum value.
     - `max`: Maximum value.

**** Example
     @code toml
     [content_schema.fields.word_count]
     type = "integer"
     min = 100
     @end

*** Float
    - Validates decimal numbers.

**** Options
     - `min`: Minimum value.
     - `max`: Maximum value.

**** Example
     @code toml
     [content_schema.fields.rating]
     type = "float"
     min = 0.0
     max = 5.0
     @end

*** Boolean
    - Validates `true` or `false` values.

**** Example
     @code toml
     [content_schema.fields.draft]
     type = "boolean"
     @end

*** Array
    - Validates lists of items.

**** Options
     - `items`: Schema for individual items.
     - `min_items`: Minimum number of items.
     - `max_items`: Maximum number of items.
     - `must_contain`: Specific values that must be present.

**** Example
     @code toml
     [content_schema.fields.categories]
     type = "array"
     items = { type = "string" }
     min_items = 1
     max_items = 5
     must_contain = ["norgolith"]
     @end

     Inline `items = { ... }` is the recommended style. An equivalent nested table (`[content_schema.fields.categories.items]`) also works, but array-of-tables syntax (`[[items]]`) is not supported: `items` takes a single field definition, not a list.

*** Object
    - Validates nested metadata structures.

**** Options
     - `schema`: Nested metadata structure schema.

**** Example
     @code toml
     [content_schema.fields.author]
     type = "object"
     schema = { name = { type = "string" }, email = { type = "string" } }
     @end

** Validation Rules
   Validation rules allow you to enforce conditional logic based on metadata values.

*** Example: Conditional Rule
    @code toml
    [content_schema.posts.rules]
    if = { draft = false }  # Condition
    then = { required = ["publish_date"] }  # Action
    @end

    This rule ensures that if `draft` is `false`, the `publish_date` field must be present in any `posts/` file.

    The `then` action supports two clauses:
    - `required`: Fields that must be present when the condition matches.
    - `fields`: Additional field definitions enforced when the condition matches, using the same options as `[content_schema.fields.*]`.

*** Example: `then.fields`
    @code toml
    [content_schema.posts.rules]
    if = { featured = true }  # Condition
    then = { fields = { hero_image = { type = "string", min_length = 10 } } }  # Action
    @end

    When `featured` is `true`, the `hero_image` field must exist and be a string of at least 10 characters in any `posts/` file.

** Schema Inheritance
   Schemas can inherit and extend rules based on content paths. For example, rules for `/posts` can extend global rules, and `/posts/2023` can further extend `/posts`.

*** Example: Path-Based Inheritance

    @code toml
    # Global rules
    [content_schema]
    required = ["title"]

    # Rules for /posts
    [content_schema.posts]
    required = ["category"]

    # Rules for /posts/2023
    [content_schema.posts.2023]
    required = ["year"]
    @end

    In this example:
    - All content requires a `title` field.
    - Posts require a `category` field.
    - Posts in `/posts/2023` require a `year` field.

*** Wildcard Paths
    Schema nodes can use `"*"` to match a single path component and `"**"` to match any number of remaining components. Precedence is exact match, then `"*"`, then `"**"`.

    @code toml
    # Applies to any single subdirectory under /posts (posts/2024, posts/2025, ...)
    [content_schema.posts."*"]
    required = ["publish_date"]

    # Applies to everything under /docs at any depth (docs/rust, docs/rust/advanced, ...)
    [content_schema.docs."**"]
    required = ["reviewer"]
    @end

    Note: `*` and `**` are not valid bare TOML keys, so they must be quoted. Schemas apply by longest matching prefix: a schema node always inherits the rules of its ancestors, and rules for a deeper node never leak to its parents.

    +html.class text-base-alt mt-12 lg:mt-14
    ___

** Practical Examples
*** Example 1: Blog Post Schema
    @code toml
    [content_schema]
    required = ["title", "author"]

    [content_schema.fields.title]
    type = "string"
    max_length = 120

    [content_schema.fields.author]
    type = "string"

    [content_schema.fields.version]
    type = "string"

    [content_schema.fields.word_count]
    type = "integer"

    [content_schema.posts.rules]
    if = { draft = false }
    then = { required = ["publish_date"] }
    @end

    This schema validates the `version` string and the numeric `word_count` for every blog post.
*** Example 2: Project Documentation Schema
    @code toml
    [content_schema]
    required = ["title", "version"]

    [content_schema.fields.title]
    type = "string"

    [content_schema.fields.project_version]
    type = "string"
    pattern = '^\d+\.\d+\.\d+$'  # Semantic versioning

    [content_schema.docs.rules]
    if = { status = "published" }
    then = { required = ["reviewer"] }
    @end

    +html.class text-base-alt mt-12 lg:mt-14
    ___

** Checking Content From the CLI
   Run `lith check` to validate every content file against the schema without building the site. Useful for CI or pre-commit hooks.
   - Exits with code 1 if any file has validation errors.
   - Prints the same diagnostics as `lith build`.
   - Warning-severity issues (near-miss field names, failing rule conditions) are reported but do not fail the check.
   - Prints "No content schema configured; nothing to check" and exits 0 when no `[content_schema]` is set.

** Troubleshooting Validation Errors
   Norgolith will show you validation issues by default in the following format, either as errors when trying to build the site or as warnings during the development server:

   @code bash
   × Schema validation failed (2 errors)
   help: Fix the listed metadata field(s) and rebuild

   Error: norgolith::schema::constraint_violation (https://norgolith.dev/docs/content-schemas/#norgolith-schema-constraint-violation)

   × Constraint violation for field 'title': Exceeds max length 12
    ╭─[ content/posts/hello.norg:2:8 ]
   1 │ @document.meta
   2 │ title: A Very Long Title That Exceeds Maximum Length
    ·        ────────────────┬────────────────
    ·                        ╰── Exceeds max length 12
   3 │ author: alice
     ╰────
   help: Exceeds max length 12
   @end

   Each error identifies the offending file and line, highlights the value that failed, and links to the matching error code reference below.

*** Error Code Reference
    Norgolith schema validation uses structured error codes. Each error has a unique miette diagnostic code, a severity level, and a machine-readable field payload.

    +html.class text-base-alt mt-12 lg:mt-14
    ___

**** `norgolith::schema::missing_field`
     - *Variant*: `MissingField { field, span }`
     - *Severity*: Error
     - *Description*: A field declared as required in the content schema is absent from the document metadata. The span points at the `@document.meta` directive, identifying the offending file.
     - *Example message*:
       @code text
       Missing field 'publish_date'
       @end
     - *Fix*: Add the missing field to the document's `@document.meta` block.

**** `norgolith::schema::type_mismatch`
    - *Variant*: `TypeMismatch { field, expected, actual, span }`
    - *Severity*: Error
    - *Description*: Metadata field value does not match the type declared in the schema (string, integer, float, boolean, array, object). The `span` (byte offset of the field value in the source) drives the source label rendered by miette.
     - *Example message*:
       @code text
       Type mismatch for field 'version': expected string, got number
       @end
     - *Fix*: Change the field value to match the expected type, or update the schema's field definition.

**** `norgolith::schema::constraint_violation`
    - *Variant*: `ConstraintViolation { field, message, span }`
    - *Severity*: Error
    - *Description*: Value passes type checking but violates a field constraint — min/max length for strings, min/max for integer/float, regex pattern, array item bounds, etc. The `span` (byte offset of the field value in the source) drives the source label rendered by miette.
     - *Example message*:
       @code text
       Constraint violation for field 'title': Exceeds max length 12
       @end
     - *Fix*: Adjust the field value to satisfy the constraint, or relax the schema definition.

**** `norgolith::schema::rule_condition`
     - *Variant*: `RuleConditionFailed { message }`
     - *Severity*: Warning
     - *Description*: A conditional validation rule's `if` condition could not be evaluated (missing condition field) or the `then` clause failed.
     - *Example message*:
       @code text
       Rule condition failed: Missing condition field 'draft'
       @end
     - *Fix*: Ensure the condition field exists in the metadata, or update the rule definition.

**** `norgolith::schema::unknown_field`
     - *Variant*: `UnknownField { field, suggested, span }`
     - *Severity*: Warning
     - *Description*: A metadata field is not defined in the content schema, but its name matches a defined field once casing and separators (`-`, `_`) are ignored. Likely a typo or kebab-case/snake-case drift, e.g. metadata `project-version` vs schema `project_version`.
     - *Example message*:
       @code text
       Unknown field 'project-version' (did you mean 'project_version'?)
       @end
     - *Fix*: Rename the metadata field to match the schema, or add it to the schema if it is intentional. Metadata keys are matched exactly and case-sensitively.
